      "texts": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "heading": "Asking about the pain",
          "body": "Pain is what the patient says it is. A score is a record of what they told you, not a judgement you make about whether the figure sounds right for the operation they have had.\n\nAsk at rest and ask again on movement. The two are often several points apart, and it is the score on movement that decides whether this patient will manage to cough, to sit out, or to walk to the door.\n\nScore the pain before the analgesia and again afterwards, at a stated interval. A dose with no second reading tells nobody whether it worked, and the next nurse begins the same experiment again.\n\nWhere a patient cannot use a number, watch instead. Grimacing, guarding, restlessness, refusing to move and a rising pulse are the signs, and a patient with dementia may show all five without saying a word.\n\nBelieve a patient whose face and behaviour disagree with their number. Some will report a low score out of politeness, or because they have decided that pain after an operation is something to be endured."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "heading": "Building the prescription",
          "body": "Relief is built in layers rather than from a single strong drug. Regular paracetamol underneath everything reduces how much of anything else is needed, and it is the layer most often forgotten.\n\nAn anti-inflammatory is added where the kidneys, the stomach and the platelets allow it. In an older patient, or one who is dehydrated, the harm outweighs the benefit more often than not.\n\nAn opioid sits on top of those layers, not instead of them. A patient receiving paracetamol regularly needs less of it, wakes sooner and moves earlier.\n\nPrescribe regularly for the first two days rather than only when asked. Pain that is allowed to build is harder to bring down than pain that was never allowed to reach that point.\n\nWrite a rescue dose as well, with a clear minimum interval. A chart that offers nothing between two regular doses forces a nurse to choose between the rules and the patient.\n\nReview the whole prescription daily and step down deliberately. A regular opioid that nobody stopped is how a patient goes home on a drug they never needed after the third day."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "heading": "Watching for harm",
          "body": "The dangerous effect of an opioid is not a low blood pressure and it is not the sickness. It is a falling respiratory rate, and that is the observation most often estimated rather than counted.\n\nSedation comes before the breathing slows. A patient who is difficult to rouse is the warning, and the right response is to withhold the next dose and tell somebody, not to wait for the next set of observations.\n\nCount the respiratory rate for a full minute in any patient on a regular opioid. Below eight is an emergency and the antidote and the team are needed at the bedside.\n\nConstipation is not a side effect that wears off, so a laxative is prescribed at the same time as the opioid rather than after the problem appears.\n\nSickness is treated rather than tolerated. A patient who is vomiting will refuse the next dose, and pain that goes untreated for that reason is recorded nowhere.\n\nCheck what the patient already takes at home. A regular opioid started here, on top of one taken there, is a common and entirely avoidable overdose."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "heading": "Beyond the drug chart",
          "body": "Position, warmth, a supported wound and a full explanation of what is about to happen all reduce pain, and none of them requires a prescription.\n\nTime the analgesia around what the patient has to do. A dose given twenty minutes before physiotherapy is worth more than the same dose given after it.\n\nIce, elevation and a firm pillow held against the wound while coughing are small measures with a disproportionate effect, and the patient can be taught all three in a minute.\n\nAnxiety and poor sleep both lower the threshold. A patient who has not slept for two nights will report a higher score for the same wound, and treating only the wound will not move the number.\n\nTell the patient the plan for the days ahead, including when the strong drugs will stop. Uncertainty about what happens next is itself a reason people ask for more.\n\nAsk what the patient believes about the medicines, because the belief will decide whether they take them.\nMany are afraid of becoming dependent on a drug prescribed for four days, and will quietly refuse the doses they most need on the first night rather than say so.\n\nInvolve the family in the same conversation where the patient agrees. A relative who has been told that a score of six is not something to be endured will ask on behalf of somebody who will not ask for themselves, and that request is often the one that gets the chart reviewed."
        }
      ],
