        {
          "id": "A",
          "heading": "While it is happening",
          "body": "Note the time before you do anything else. Everything that follows is decided by how long this has been going on, and nobody remembers accurately afterwards.\n\nDo not restrain the patient and do not put anything into the mouth. Neither protects the tongue, and both cause injuries that the seizure itself would not have caused.\n\nMove the furniture rather than the person. Put something soft under the head, loosen anything tight at the neck, and clear the space around them.\n\nOnce the movements stop, turn the patient onto their side and open the airway. Give oxygen and check the blood glucose at that point in every patient, without exception.\n\nWatch what actually happens rather than interpreting it. Where it began, whether the eyes turned to one side, whether both sides were involved, and how long each phase lasted are what the neurologist will ask for and what nobody else in the room will have recorded.\n\nStay with the patient. Somebody who wakes surrounded by strangers and no explanation is frightened by the recovery as much as by the event."
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "heading": "When it does not stop",
          "body": "A convulsion lasting more than five minutes will usually not stop on its own, and it is treated as an emergency from that point rather than watched for longer.\n\nThe first drug is a benzodiazepine, given by the route available. A second dose may be given after ten minutes, and no more than two doses in total should be given before moving on.\n\nBeyond two doses the risk shifts from the seizure to the breathing. Repeated benzodiazepines are the commonest reason a patient with a prolonged convulsion stops breathing.\n\nWhere two doses have not worked, a second agent is started and the anaesthetic team is called at the same time rather than afterwards.\n\nLook for the cause while the treatment runs. A low glucose, a low sodium, an infection, alcohol withdrawal, a head injury and missed medication account for most of what is found.\n\nCorrect a low glucose immediately and do not wait for a laboratory result to confirm what the meter has already shown."
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "heading": "After the event",
          "body": "Confusion afterwards is expected and may last an hour or longer. It is not another seizure and it is not a reason to give more medication.\n\nWeakness on one side that persists after the confusion clears is not expected, and it is reported the same day rather than being attributed to the event.\n\nExamine the patient for injuries once they are settled. A shoulder, the tongue and the back of the head are the sites most often hurt and the ones least often looked at.\n\nAsk about the days beforehand rather than only the minutes. Missed doses, a night without sleep, a new medicine and a recent illness are the triggers people can actually act on.\n\nWhere this is a first seizure, refer to a specialist clinic and explain that a diagnosis of epilepsy is not made from one event.\n\nTell the patient not to drive and explain plainly that the rules are set nationally and that you cannot vary them. This conversation is easier when it is had once, clearly, than when it is left for somebody else."
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "heading": "Living with it",
          "body": "Record every seizure in a diary with the date, the length and what was happening beforehand. The pattern in that diary changes treatment more often than any test does.\n\nTake the medication at the same times daily. Missed doses are the commonest reason a controlled condition stops being controlled, and the reasons for missing them are usually practical rather than deliberate.\n\nNever stop or change the dose because the seizures have stopped. That is the treatment working, not the condition going away.\n\nTeach the family what to do and, just as importantly, what not to do. Most of the harm at home comes from well-meant restraint and from things pushed into the mouth.\n\nDiscuss showers rather than baths, and discuss swimming with somebody present. These are the ordinary risks that matter, and they are manageable rather than forbidding.\n\nAsk any patient who could become pregnant to seek advice before, not after. Some of these medicines need changing well ahead of time.\n\nAsk about work and about school, and ask specifically what the patient has told the people around them.\nSomebody who has told nobody at their workplace is relying on strangers to act correctly during an event they have never been warned about.\n\nWarn about the medicines that can be bought or prescribed elsewhere. Several ordinary treatments lower the threshold or interfere with the levels, and the pharmacist is the person most likely to catch this if the condition is written on the record.\n\nAsk how the patient feels about the diagnosis, not only how the seizures are. Fear of the next event keeps more people at home than the events themselves do."
        }
